  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1158 PM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nNo major changes have been made to the previous forecast. Fog is \nstill expected to spread west later tonight and into Saturday \nmorning as low level moisture gets wrapped around a surface low \nnorth of Denver. The fog could be dense at times, lowering \nvisibilities to less than 1/4 mile over Denver, the I-25 corridor, \nparts of Lincoln County, and the surrounding plains. The fog is less \nlikely to reach the higher terrain of the Palmer Divide and if it \ndoes, it should be more patchy and shorter-lived. \n\nA shortwave trough will bring a surge of mid level moisture to much \nof the state on Saturday which will help promote mountain snow in \nafternoon and evening. Flow at the 700mb level will be west to \nnorthwest, favoring orographic lift for the central mountains and \nthe highest peaks of the Park Range. Model soundings sampled over \nvarious mountain ranges in our area show relatively deep moist \nprofiles with saturation along the dendritic growth zone. However, \ngiven the weak state of the shortwave as it slides over our area and \nlackluster synoptic forcing for ascent, snowfall is expected to be \nlight orographic in nature. Accumulations in the 1 to 3 inch range \nwill be possible for elevations above 10kft, with locally higher \namounts above 12kt. Mountain valleys could also see some snow \nshowers throughout the afternoon, but no accumulation is expected \ngiven the marginal surface and air temperatures (highs in the 40s). \nFor the Denver metro and east CO plains, fog may be slow to erode \nand scattered low clouds could persist through early afternoon. \nDespite the abundant moisture at the lowest levels of the \natmosphere, model soundings for Denver show a stubborn dry layer at \naround the 700mb level. Any showers that try to develop over the \nDenver area will initially struggle to reach the ground so we have \nlimited the better PoPs (17%-40%) to the late afternoon and \novernight hours. If showers are able to linger into the early \nmorning hours of Sunday (~30% chance), a few snowflakes to even a \nlight dusting of snow cannot be ruled out over the crest of the \nPalmer Divide. Otherwise, patchy fog will be possible once again \nSunday morning over the parts of the Denver metro and CO plains. \n\nConditions dry out state-wide on Sunday afternoon as an upper level \nridge begins to build over the Four Corners. This ridge will warm \ntemperatures back up to well above average for the season Monday \nthrough Tuesday, with afternoon highs in the low 60s for the plains, \nand 50s at the mountain valleys. Tuesday afternoon could be breezy \nas the pressure gradient tightens ahead of the next system and \nhigher wind speeds aloft from the jet positioned over the area mix \ndown to the surface.\n\nA shortwave trough is still on track to bring mountain snow on \nWednesday with the Denver metro and east CO plains remaining dry. \nWhile there is still quite a bit of uncertainty with this system, at \nleast a couple of inches of snow accumulation seem reasonable for \nthe central and northern mountains. Beyond Wednesday, model \nensembles and deterministic runs show fundamental differences with \nevery run cycle. Perhaps the only \"change\" has been a slight trend \ntowards cooler solutions that bring a cutoff low to the Four Corners \nregion sometime late next week. At this time, ~40% of the NBM \nmembers show at least one inch of snow for Denver within a 72 hour \nperiod. There is also little to no agreement in the timing of this \nsystem, with some solutions bringing the cutoff low into the area as \nearly as Friday morning, while others push this system back to \nTuesday of Thanksgiving week. What we do know at this time is that \ntemperatures in long-range ensembles keep trending at or below \naverage for the long term (days 8-15), increasing our confidence \nin a colder end to November.",
